const body = `<h1>Unit 9: Inheritance</h1>
<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2 material.</strong> Inheritance is no longer on the AP exam
    (the 2025 CED removed it). We teach it AFTER the May 12 exam, as the engine
    room of the RPG: it is how Player and Monster become one family, and how
    your battle engine handles every creature type with one loop. If you are
    here before May, you are ahead of schedule. Nice.
</div>



<p>
    You've written Player.java. You've written Monster.java. They have different names,
    different stats, different behavior. But they share a lot of the same code: hit points,
    attack methods, name fields. Copy-paste is the enemy of good code.
</p>

<p>
    This unit introduces <strong>inheritance</strong> — the mechanism that lets one class
    inherit everything from another and then add its own specialties. Player and Monster
    both extend a shared GameCharacter class. Method overriding lets each subclass change
    how it attacks. Polymorphism lets you treat a Skeleton, Dragon, and Goblin all as
    the same type — a collection of GameCharacter objects.
</p>

<h2>Concepts</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Topic</th><th>What you'll learn</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="extends-and-super.html">extends &amp; super</a></td>
        <td>Creating subclasses. Calling parent constructors and methods.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="overriding.html">Method Overriding</a></td>
        <td>Replacing a parent method with specialized behavior.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="abstract-classes.html">Abstract Classes</a></td>
        <td>Forcing subclasses to implement specific methods.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="polymorphism.html">Polymorphism</a></td>
        <td>Treating multiple types as one. Polymorphic arrays and ArrayLists.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="object-interfaces-casting.html">Object, Interfaces &amp; Casting</a></td>
        <td>The Object class (toString, equals). Interfaces as contracts. Safe casting with instanceof.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>Your RPG Project</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Guide</th><th>What you'll build</th></tr>
    <tr>
        <td><a href="character-hierarchy.html">GameCharacter + Refactored Player/Monster (Student RPG Guide)</a></td>
        <td>Extract everything Player and Monster share into a GameCharacter superclass. Player and Monster become subclasses. Create specialized enemy types — Skeleton, Dragon, Goblin — each with their own overridden attack method. Polymorphic combat: one ArrayList&lt;GameCharacter&gt; that holds your hero AND all enemies.</td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2>RPG Thread</h2>

<p>
    This is the unification unit. Every class you've built across Units 1-8 gets organized
    into a hierarchy. Player and Monster share GameCharacter. Inventory items become subclasses
    of Item (Weapon, Armor, Potion). In Unit 10, recursive AoE spells target everything on
    the board — and because of polymorphism, one method handles Skeletons, Dragons, and
    Players identically.
</p>

<h2>Prerequisites</h2>

<p>Before starting this unit, you should be comfortable with:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Writing classes, constructors, and methods: <a href="../unit-05/index.html">Unit 5</a></li>
    <li>Arrays and ArrayList: <a href="../unit-06/index.html">Unit 6</a>, <a href="../unit-07/index.html">Unit 7</a></li>
    <li>2D arrays: <a href="../unit-08/index.html">Unit 8</a></li>
    <li>Method signatures, return types, and parameters</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reference Materials</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Quick Reference:</strong> <a href="../reference/unit-09-quickref.html">Inheritance Quick Ref</a> &mdash; extends, super, @Override, abstract, polymorphic declarations</li>
</ul>

<h2>Assessment</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Part A:</strong> Knowledge — inheritance terminology, super(), overriding, abstract, polymorphism</li>
    <li><strong>Part B:</strong> Code trace — predict method dispatch in inheritance hierarchies</li>
    <li><strong>Part C:</strong> Write code — implement a class hierarchy from a specification</li>
</ul>`;
